# Byte-compiled / optimized / DLL files
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Distribution / packaging
build/
develop-eggs/
dist/
eggs/
.eggs/
lib/
lib64/
parts/
wheels/
*.egg-info/
*.egg

# Virtual Environments
.venv/
env/
venv/
ENV/

# Testing
htmlcov/
.tox/
.coverage
.pytest_cache/

# IDE/Editor
# Contents, not the directory itself: git will not re-include a file whose
# PARENT is excluded, so `.vscode/` followed by `!.vscode/mcp.json` silently
# keeps ignoring it. Excluding the contents leaves the negation reachable.
.vscode/*
# ...except the MCP config, which is how Copilot reaches the Métis server.
# `.github/agents/` ships thirteen agents whose `tools:` frontmatter names MCP
# tools, and without this file Copilot loads every one of them declaring tools
# it has no way to call. Committed rather than left user-local because it needs
# no absolute path — `uv run --directory` resolves from the repository root, so
# the same file works on every clone. (The sibling project gitignores its copy
# because its equivalent carries machine-specific paths.)
!.vscode/mcp.json
.idea/
.ipynb_checkpoints

# Environment variables
.env

# Local workspace state
.DS_Store
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
.claude/settings.local.json
*.work

# Workflow run records — runtime state, not source. A halted run is durable on
# the machine that started it; committing one would replay another machine's
# position and its input fingerprints.
metis-server/.metis/runs/

# The rest of $METIS_HOME, for the same reason — and this list was one entry
# long when it needed to be four. Two runtime artefacts were committed:
#
#   .metis/storage/          a graph export whose manifest declared 65 labels
#                            against an ontology of 43, and 8 lessons against 17.
#                            1,050 lines of Cypher describing a schema that no
#                            longer exists — the exact class of plausible stale
#                            artefact docs/historical/ was deleted for.
#   .metis/reviews/          review output; the committed one was decided by
#                            nobody and read by nothing.
#
# `storage.storage_dir()` is `<repo>/.metis/storage`, so this is not only about
# Métis's own checkout: every project Métis analyses grows one of these inside
# it, and without these lines each of them commits its own graph export.
.metis/storage/
.metis/reviews/
.metis/runs/
.metis/cache/

# The rendered academy (`metis academy`). Generated, and unlike `docs/guide/` it
# is NOT committed — the difference is what each one is for. The guide is
# reference material a reader of the repository should see current, so it is
# checked in and `metis guide --check` fails on a diff. This is a rendering of
# lessons that are already here in their source form, and every page inlines a
# ~90KB search index; committing it would put that in every diff that touches a
# lesson, to show nobody anything they could not read in `docs/academy/`.
docs/academy-site/

# Publication baselines — the same argument. `<model>.published.json` records
# what THIS deployment last published, written only after a successful
# publication (drift.record_generation). Committing one would tell another
# machine that cases were published which never left it, and three-way drift
# would then read a real manual edit as unchanged.
*.published.json

# Joern's scratch workspace. It writes one into the working directory whenever a
# script runs, holding a copy of the CPG — binary, large, and reproducible from
# the source it was built from. `code_analysis.engine` keeps the artefacts it
# actually reuses under the target repo's .metis/cache/ instead.
workspace/

# Generated automation artefacts and anything else scratch. `metis generate
# --out tmp/testcases/<model>` writes here.
#
# **Not committed, deliberately.** A generated suite is derived from an approved
# model the same way the database is derived from `graph/` — regenerate it and
# you have it back, and committing one invites a hand-edit that the next
# regeneration silently discards. The emitters say so in every file header ("Do
# not hand-edit: regenerate after the model changes"), and a tracked copy would
# make that instruction unenforceable.
tmp/
